    rationale: `This tests understanding of POST-TRANSFUSION COMPLICATIONS and MULTIFACTORIAL ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY. Clinical reasoning: (1) This patient: Sepsis + hemorrhage + massive transfusion. Multi-hit model for AKI. (2) Risk factors for AKI visible in labs: (a) Hematocrit 24% (still low despite transfusion = ongoing bleeding or hemodilution). (b) Potassium 5.9 (elevated—danger zone). Normal <5.0. >6.0 risks cardiac arrhythmias. (c) pH 7.22 (moderate acidosis). (d) Calcium 6.5 (low—citrate toxicity from transfusion). (e) Oliguria (0.3 mL/kg/hr; normal >1 mL/kg/hr = AKI indicator). (3) AKI etiology (multiple): (a) Sepsis-induced AKI: Sepsis causes systemic inflammation, vasoconstriction of renal arteries, reduced GFR. (b) Hemorrhagic shock-induced AKI: From initial bleeding, even if transfused, can still have inadequate renal perfusion. (c) Transfusion-induced: (i) Citrate toxicity: FFP/blood products contain citrate anticoagulant. Citrate binds calcium → hypocalcemia. Calcium needed for renal vascular tone and glomerular filtration. (ii) Hyperkalemia: Stored blood has high K. Released from hemolysis. Hyperkalemia impairs renal function and risks cardiac arrhythmias. (iii) Acidosis: Transfused blood is acidotic. Acidosis reduces renal perfusion and GFR. (4) Oliguria is ABNORMAL and concerning. Urine output is best real-time indicator of renal perfusion in ICU. (5) Management: (a) AGGRESSIVE fluids: Bolus normal saline or balanced crystalloid to improve renal perfusion. BUT must monitor for overload (crackles, JVD, pulmonary edema). (b) CALCIUM: Calcium gluconate 10-20 mL 10% IV if hypocalcemia severe (cardiac effects, arrhythmias). Replaces citrate-bound calcium. (c) POTASSIUM management: Hyperkalemia emergency. (i) EKG: Check for peaked T waves (cardiac danger sign). (ii) Calcium gluconate (membrane stabilization). (iii) Insulin + glucose (shifts K intracellularly). (iv) Sodium bicarbonate (counteract acidosis, shift K in). (v) Loop diuretic if urine output improves (promotes K excretion). (d) ACID-BASE: Address acidosis (improve perfusion, reduce lactate generation). (e) MONITOR: Hourly urine output, continuous cardiac monitoring, ABG/electrolytes q1-2H. (f) DIALYSIS standby: If K+ continues rising or creatinine worsens, prepare for emergent dialysis. (6) Prognosis: Sepsis-related AKI + massive transfusion AKI = high mortality if not managed aggressively. (7) Option A (recovering well): WRONG. Oliguria is concerning sign. (8) Option C (sepsis resolving): Sepsis is only 2 hours into treatment. Hasn't resolved. Still at risk. (9) Option D (oliguria normal): WRONG. AKI is abnormal and requires intervention. (10) Option B is correct.`,
