    rationale: `This tests understanding of TRANSFUSION-TRANSMITTED BACTERIAL INFECTION and blood product storage considerations. Clinical reasoning: (1) Transfusion-transmitted infections: (a) Viral: HIV, hepatitis B/C, HTLV (screened; rare now). (b) BACTERIAL: Surprisingly COMMON transfusion-transmitted infection. (c) Parasitic: Malaria, Chagas disease (rare in US, more in developing countries). (d) Fungal: Rare, usually Candida. (2) Bacterial contamination sources: (a) Pre-transfusion unit contamination (donor bacteremia at time of collection—rare). (b) Skin flora contamination during collection (more common). (c) Environmental contamination during processing. (d) Post-collection contamination during storage. (3) Storage conditions and bacterial risk: (a) COLD storage (1-6°C) for PRBCs: Most bacteria DON'T grow well in cold. Gram-negative more likely than gram-positive in cold storage. Listeria monocytogenes (rare) can grow in cold. Overall risk ~1-2 in 100,000 units. (b) ROOM TEMPERATURE storage (used for platelets, fresh plasma): MUCH higher bacterial risk. Platelets stored 20-24°C for only 5 days (vs. 42 days for RBCs). Bacterial growth rapid at room temperature. Higher transfusion-related bacterial infection risk with platelets. (c) FROZEN storage (FFP): Minimal bacterial risk (bacteria don't grow when frozen). (d) CELL SALVAGE (autologous blood collected intra-op): Room temperature, collected from patient, reinfused back to patient. Usually considered low-risk IF aseptically collected. BUT if not processed immediately or contaminated during collection → high bacterial risk. (4) This patient's presentation: (a) Fever, chills, jaundice, positive blood cultures within hours post-op. (b) Gram-negative bacteria. (c) Temporal relationship to transfusions. (5) Investigation: (a) Which product most likely source? Cell salvage (room temp) > Platelets (if given, room temp) > FFP (frozen, low risk) > PRBCs (cold, low risk). (b) Blood culture identifies organism: Gram-negative suggests Pseudomonas (common in room-temp contamination), E. coli (GI source), Klebsiella. (c) Review: Was cell salvage processed aseptically? Time from collection to reinfusion? Were sterile field breaches during collection? (6) TTBI presentation: Can be fulminant—fever, sepsis, shock within hours. Mortality high (15-20%) if not treated rapidly. (7) Management: (a) STOP further transfusion from suspicious unit. (b) Blood cultures (guide antibiotics). (c) Broad-spectrum antibiotics STAT pending culture results. (d) Supportive care (fluids, pressors if shock). (e) Investigate blood bank protocols. (8) Prevention: (a) Proper aseptic technique during blood collection. (b) Skin antisepsis. (c) Diversion of first mL (discard) to remove skin flora. (d) Bacterial detection systems in blood bank. (e) Appropriate storage conditions. (9) Option A (all equally risky): WRONG. Storage temperature dramatically affects bacterial growth risk. (10) Option C (sterile, cannot transmit): WRONG. Bacterial contamination possible. (11) Option D (only immunocompromised): WRONG. TTBI can affect immunocompetent patients, though immunocompromised at higher risk for severe disease. (12) Option B is correct.`,
