    rationale: `This tests understanding of TRANSFUSION PHYSIOLOGY and EXPECTED HEMOGLOBIN INCREMENT. Clinical reasoning: (1) "Rule of thumb" for transfusion: Each unit PRBCs should raise Hemoglobin ~1 g/dL in adult. BUT this is AVERAGE case. Many factors affect actual increment. (2) This patient's factors: (a) Chronic hemoglobin baseline 10 g/dL from chronic bleeding. His body ADAPTED to chronically low Hgb. Not in acute shock. So transfusion not life-saving, just optimization. (b) Chronic kidney disease (eGFR 25 = severe CKD): Kidneys produce erythropoietin (EPO), which stimulates RBC production. CKD → reduced EPO → baseline lower Hgb expected. This is CHRONIC adaptation. (c) ONE unit transfusion in patient already at 10 g/dL baseline has MODEST effect. (d) Ongoing bleeding: If patient continues to bleed post-op (even small amount), offsets transfusion. (e) Hemodilution: IV fluids given post-op dilute blood → Hgb diluted out. (f) Timing of labs: If labs drawn immediately after transfusion, RBCs not yet fully equilibrated in circulation. (3) Expected Hgb post-transfusion: Pre-transfusion 10 g/dL + ~0.8 g/dL increment = ~10.8 g/dL. This PATIENT got 10.8—so actually as expected! (4) Why not "more units immediately"? (a) Transfusion has risks (immune reactions, infection, iron overload). (b) Patient doing well hemodynamically (stable vitals, urine output adequate). (c) Chronic low Hgb patient doesn't NEED Hgb of 13-14. Can function at 10-11. (d) Over-transfusion in CKD patient risks volume overload (pulmonary edema). (5) Transfusion triggers for this patient: (a) SYMPTOMATIC: Dyspnea, tachycardia >110, mental status change, chest pain. (b) HEMOGLOBIN-based: <7-8 g/dL in stable patient (higher threshold <10 if unstable/cardiac disease). (c) This patient at 10.8 post-op, stable = no further transfusion needed now. (6) Monitoring: Serial Hgb checks (daily post-op) to track trend. If falling despite no ongoing bleeding → investigate. If stable/rising → good sign. (7) CKD-specific: May eventually need EPO (erythropoiesis-stimulating agent) to help RBC production, but not acutely post-op. (8) Option A (ineffective, need more): WRONG. Transfusion worked as expected. Over-transfusion risks. (9) Option C (refusal): No evidence of refusal documented. (10) Option D (no blood loss from hip replacement): WRONG. Hip replacement involves cutting through bone, periosteum, vasculature—definitely causes blood loss. (11) Option B is correct.`,
