    correct: [0, 1, 3, 5],
    rationale: `This SATA tests comprehensive management of massive transfusion complications. Analysis: (1) STATEMENT A (Calcium gluconate): CORRECT—Calcium serves dual purpose: (a) Replaces citrate-bound calcium (hypocalcemia from FFP/blood products). (b) Stabilizes cardiac membrane against hyperkalemia. If severe hyperkalemia + peaked T waves on EKG → calcium gluconate STAT. (2) STATEMENT B (Active rewarming): CORRECT—Hypothermia is part of lethal triad. Core temp 34.2°C severely impairs coagulation (each 1°C drop ~7% worse coagulopathy). Rewarming: Warm fluids (use blood warmers), warming blankets, heating lamps, extracorporeal rewarming (ECMO/bypass) if available and severe. Don't assess coagulation labs until temp >35°C (results invalid in severe hypothermia). (3) STATEMENT C (High-dose sodium bicarbonate): POTENTIALLY CORRECT but requires caution—pH 7.12 is severe acidosis and treatment appropriate. But sodium bicarbonate is NOT emergency single intervention. Use COMBINED approach: improve perfusion (reduces lactate), small-medium doses of bicarbonate (50-100 mEq over time), not "high-dose STAT." High-dose NaHCO3 risks hypernatremia, hypokalemia initially (shifts K in paradoxically), volume overload. Stated as "rapidly correct" is overstated. Include but requires nuance. (4) STATEMENT D (Balanced transfusion 1:1:1): CORRECT—Current guidelines recommend balanced transfusion ratios (1 PRBC : 1 FFP : 1 platelet) for massive transfusion vs. old practice of high PRBC ratios. Point-of-care testing (TEG/ROTEM) superior to standard labs for guiding therapy. Continue transfusion if ongoing hemorrhage documented. (5) STATEMENT E (Restrict fluids): INCORRECT—Paradoxical statement. Massive transfusion patient may NEED more fluids for: (a) Ongoing bleeding replacement. (b) Urine output maintenance (prevent AKI). (c) Organ perfusion. YES, must watch for overload, but "restrict" is wrong approach. Liberal (not excessive) fluid resuscitation appropriate in hemorrhage. (6) STATEMENT F (Insulin + dextrose for hyperkalemia): CORRECT—Standard treatment for hyperkalemia. Insulin 10 units IV + 25g dextrose shifts K+ into cells, lowering serum K+ temporarily. Works synergistically with calcium (membrane stabilization) and bicarbonate (shift K+ in). Triple therapy is standard. (7) CORRECT ANSWERS: A, B, D, F. These address lethal triad (hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy) and hyperkalemia management. Statements C (overstated) and E (contraindicated restriction) are incorrect.`,
